{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790290399,"nonce":"DX4e1N8fXjJQkWdLdBFbkKQCIWc=","title":"LLVM tier compiles float != as an ordered compare, so NaN != NaN is false","message":"The LLVM backend lowers BinaryOp::Ne on floats and BinaryOp::FNe with FloatPredicate::ONE (ordered and not equal), which is false when either operand is NaN. Cranelift lowers the same ops with FloatCC::NotEqual (unordered or not equal), which is true for NaN, as IEEE, Lua and Python require.\n\nVisible in Lua: zl_rawset checks `f ~= f` to raise \"table index is NaN\". Once zl_rawset is promoted to the LLVM tier (key_kinds.lua stores 6001 float keys first) the check never fires, and `t[0/0] = 1` stores a NaN key. tables/key_kinds.lua fails about 1 run in 15 under load with ZYLUA_LLVM=1 and the compile worker on (base 8844ca79 too); Cranelift only, 0 in 90. Found merging C-baseline-first-loop-free, whose runner change runs the Lua suite with the worker on.\n\nFix: FloatPredicate::UNE for both.","files":null}]}